---
// The privacy policy, which both stores require before an app can ship.
//
// **Written from the code rather than from a template.** Google cross-checks
// this against the Data Safety form and rejects mismatches, and a boilerplate
// policy would claim things this app does not do — analytics it does not
// have, servers it does not talk to — while missing the one fact that
// actually matters: the default homeserver is operated by Super Jackfruit
// Labs, so by default your messages do reach our infrastructure.
//
// Every claim below was checked against the source on 2026-09-14:
//
//   no analytics      nothing matching posthog/sentry/analytics/telemetry/
//                     firebase in src/lib, the core, or either native host
//   credentials       crates/supermessage-core/src/secrets.rs — the OS secret
//                     store, holding `matrix_session` and `homeserver_url`
//   federation        `allow_federation = false` in /etc/tuwunel/tuwunel.toml
//                     on the host. The policy previously described federation
//                     as something that happens here. It does not.
//   default server    LoginView.swift's `@AppStorage("login.homeserver")`
//                     defaults to https://id.agentpod.dev, and the field is a
//                     TextField the reader can change
//
// If any of those change, this page becomes wrong.
//
// **The contact address is on superjackfruit.com, not supermessage.dev, and
// that is not an oversight.** `supermessage.dev` publishes no MX records at
// all, so mail to it bounces at the DNS layer; `superjackfruit.com` routes to
// Google Workspace. A policy naming an address that cannot receive mail is
// worse than one naming no address, because both stores treat it as the
// deletion route and a user gets silence.
import '../styles/tokens.css';
import ShareMeta from '../components/ShareMeta.astro';

const title = 'Privacy — supermessage';
const description =
  'What supermessage stores, what leaves your device, and who can see it.';

const updated = '14 September 2026';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>{title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    <ShareMeta title={title} description={description} />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <link
      rel="stylesheet"
      href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Bricolage+Grotesque:opsz,wght@12..96,400;12..96,600;12..96,800&family=IBM+Plex+Sans:wght@400;500;600&display=swap"
    />
    <style>
      :root {
        --display: 'Bricolage Grotesque', 'Trebuchet MS', sans-serif;
        --body: 'IBM Plex Sans', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', sans-serif;
      }

      * { box-sizing: border-box; }

      body {
        margin: 0;
        background: var(--color-surface-sunken);
        color: var(--color-content);
        font-family: var(--body);
        font-size: 17px;
        line-height: 1.6;
        -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
      }

      main {
        max-width: 68ch;
        margin: 0 auto;
        padding: 48px 24px 96px;
      }

      a { color: var(--color-accent); }

      h1 {
        font-family: var(--display);
        font-size: clamp(32px, 6vw, 44px);
        line-height: 1.1;
        margin: 0 0 8px;
      }

      h2 {
        font-family: var(--display);
        font-size: 22px;
        margin: 40px 0 8px;
      }

      .updated { color: var(--color-content-muted); margin: 0 0 40px; }

      /* The one claim a reader is most likely to be surprised by, so it is
         the one thing on the page given a surface of its own. */
      .callout {
        background: var(--color-surface);
        border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
        border-left: 3px solid var(--color-signal);
        border-radius: 8px;
        padding: 16px 20px;
        margin: 24px 0;
      }

      table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 16px 0; }
      th, td {
        text-align: left;
        padding: 10px 12px;
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
        vertical-align: top;
      }
      th { font-weight: 600; }
      td:first-child { white-space: nowrap; }

      footer {
        margin-top: 56px;
        padding-top: 24px;
        border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
        color: var(--color-content-muted);
        font-size: 15px;
      }
    </style>
  </head>

  <body>
    <main>
      <h1>Privacy</h1>
      <p class="updated">Last updated {updated}</p>

      <p>
        supermessage is a <a href="https://matrix.org">Matrix</a> client. Almost everything it
        does happens between your device and a Matrix homeserver — so the honest version of this
        policy is mostly about <em>which</em> homeserver, and what we can see when it is ours.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>The app ships pointing at a homeserver we run.</strong> The login screen defaults
        to <code>id.agentpod.dev</code>, which is operated by Super Jackfruit Labs. If you sign in
        there, your messages, room memberships and media pass through and are stored on our
        infrastructure, in the same way they would on any Matrix homeserver. The homeserver
        address is an editable field: point it at your own server and we receive nothing.
      </div>

      <h2>What the app stores on your device</h2>

      <table>
        <tr>
          <th>What</th>
          <th>Where</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Your session and homeserver address</td>
          <td>The operating system's secret store — Keychain on iOS and macOS, Credential
            Manager on Windows, Secret Service on Linux, and the Android keystore.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Messages, rooms and cached media</td>
          <td>A local database, so the app works offline and does not refetch what it already
            has.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Preferences</td>
          <td>Ordinary app storage — which room you had open, appearance, and similar.</td>
        </tr>
      </table>

      <p>
        Signing out clears the session. Removing the app removes the rest.
      </p>

      <h2>What we collect</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>The app contains no analytics, telemetry, crash reporting or advertising
        software.</strong> There is no third-party SDK in it that reports on what you do. We do
        not build a profile of you, and there is nothing to sell.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you use our homeserver, it keeps what a Matrix homeserver keeps in order to work: your
        account, the rooms you are in, the messages and media in them, and ordinary server logs
        including IP addresses. If you use a different homeserver, whoever runs it holds that
        instead, under their policy rather than this one.
      </p>

      <h2>Encryption</h2>

      <p>
        Traffic between the app and a homeserver is encrypted in transit. For rooms with Matrix
        end-to-end encryption enabled, the homeserver — including ours — cannot read message
        contents. Rooms without it are readable by the server that stores them, which is true of
        Matrix generally and worth knowing rather than assuming.
      </p>

      <h2>Who else receives anything</h2>

      <p>
        The people and agents in your rooms, through the homeserver, and nobody else. We do not
        share data with advertisers or data brokers.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Our homeserver does not federate.</strong> Matrix normally copies messages to
        every server whose users share a room with you; <code>id.agentpod.dev</code> runs with
        federation switched off, so nothing you send there reaches another homeserver at all.
        The trade runs both ways: you can only talk to people and agents on that server. If you
        point the app at a different homeserver, whatever that one does applies instead.
      </p>

      <p>
        This site is served through Cloudflare, which processes request logs to deliver it. The
        site sets no cookies and runs no analytics.
      </p>

      <h2>Your choices</h2>

      <table>
        <tr>
          <td>Use another homeserver</td>
          <td>Change the address on the login screen. Nothing reaches us.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Leave a room</td>
          <td>Stops new messages reaching you there. Messages already sent remain with the
            server and its members, as on any Matrix server.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Delete your account</td>
          <td>Email us and we will deactivate it on our homeserver. There is no in-app sign-up,
            so there is no in-app deletion.</td>
        </tr>
      </table>

      <h2>Children</h2>

      <p>
        supermessage is not directed at children under 13, and we do not knowingly hold data
        about them.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>

      <p>
        If this policy changes in a way that affects what we collect, we will update the date at
        the top. The page is part of the application's source, so its history is public.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>

      <p>
        Super Jackfruit Labs (OPC) Private Limited —
        <a href="mailto:privacy@superjackfruit.com">privacy@superjackfruit.com</a>
      </p>

      <footer>
        <a href="/">← supermessage</a>
      </footer>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
